{"cuisine/japanese-chinese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/japanese-chinese-cuisine","n":"Japanese Chinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in Japan","intro":[["Japanese Chinese cuisine, known as chūka ryōri or simply chūka, refers to Japanese-style Chinese dishes and the restaurants in Japan that serve them. It represents a unique fusion of Japanese and Chinese culinary traditions that have evolved since the late 19th century. This style of food is considered distinct from the \"authentic Chinese food\" available in Japan, though it retains strong influences from various Chinese regional cuisines, and there is considerable overlap between the two."],["A significant number of these dishes were introduced to Japan either by Chinese immigrants or Japanese colonizers returning from the Second Sino-Japanese war in China. This style of cuisine has found its expression in three main types of restaurants: ramen restaurants, dim sum houses, and standard Chinese-style restaurants. Most Japanese Chinese dishes have roots in Cantonese cuisine. Adaptations of Sichuanese cuisine were first introduced to Japan by celebrity chef Chen Kenmin."],["The shippoku culinary style of Nagasaki is heavily influenced by Chinese cuisine, but it is not classified as Japanese Chinese cuisine, as it originated in the 17th century, predating the development of modern Japanese Chinese cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"japanese-chinese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_Chinese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":3,"rows":[{"name":"Sudachi Recipes: Wafu Pasta","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"sudachirecipes.com","note":"Chuka recipes such as gyoza, ebi mayo and tantanmen from Japanese cook Yuto Omura.","url":"https://sudachirecipes.com/chuka-ryori-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's collection of chuka dishes as cooked in Japanese homes.","url":"https://www.justonecookbook.com/japanese-chinese-food-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"No Recipes: Japanese Chinese","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"norecipes.com","note":"Japanese Chinese recipe category on Marc Matsumoto's food site.","url":"https://norecipes.com/recipe/cuisine/japanese-chinese/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Chahan (dish)","dish/chahan-dish"],["Narutomaki","dish/narutomaki"],["Ramen shop","dish/ramen-shop"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Surimi","ingredient/surimi"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Japanese Chinese cooking","count":1,"links":[["Shippoku","cuisine/shippoku"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/60/%E3%83%A9%E3%83%BC%E3%83%A1%E3%83%B3%E5%A4%A7%E5%B0%86%E5%9B%9E%E9%8D%8B%E8%82%89%E5%AE%9A%E9%A3%9F.jpg/500px-%E3%83%A9%E3%83%BC%E3%83%A1%E3%83%B3%E5%A4%A7%E5%B0%86%E5%9B%9E%E9%8D%8B%E8%82%89%E5%AE%9A%E9%A3%9F.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"ノボホショコロトソ","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E3%83%A9%E3%83%BC%E3%83%A1%E3%83%B3%E5%A4%A7%E5%B0%86%E5%9B%9E%E9%8D%8B%E8%82%89%E5%AE%9A%E9%A3%9F.jpg"}},"cuisine/list-of-sino-mauritian-dishes":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/list-of-sino-mauritian-dishes","n":"Mauritian Chinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["This is a list of Chinese and Chinese-influenced dishes in Sino-Mauritian cuisine:"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"list-of-sino-mauritian-dishes"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Sino-Mauritian_dishes"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/faroese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/faroese-cuisine","n":"Faroese cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Danish cuisine","cuisine/danish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Denmark","intro":[["Important parts of Faroese cuisine are lamb and also fish owing to proximity to the sea. Traditional foods from the Faroe Islands include skerpikjøt (a type of dried mutton), seafood, whale meat, blubber, garnatálg, Atlantic puffins, potatoes, and few fresh vegetables."],["Much of the taste of this traditional country food is determined by the food preservation methods used; brine, drying, and the maturing of meat and fish, called ræstkjøt and ræstur fiskur."],["Animal products dominate Faroese cuisine. Popular taste has developed, however, to become closer to the European norm, and consumption of vegetables has greatly increased in recent decades while consumption of fish has diminished. Fresh and ræst lamb meat remains very popular while traditional meat products, such as various types of sausages, have lost much of their appeal with younger generations."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"faroese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faroese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Danish cuisine","cuisine/danish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Koks: Faroe Islands Flavour","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Poul Andrias Ziska, Tim Ecott","note":"Book from the Faroese restaurant Koks on traditional Faroese ingredients and techniques.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Danish cuisine","cuisine/danish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/6a/Tvost_og_spik.jpg/500px-Tvost_og_spik.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Arne List","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tvost_og_spik.jpg"}},"dish/hangtown-fry":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/hangtown-fry","n":"Hangtown fry","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Hangtown fry is a type of omelette made famous during the California Gold Rush in the 1850s. The most common version includes bacon and oysters combined with eggs, and fried together."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-hangtown-fry"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hangtown_fry"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cuisine of California","cuisine/cuisine-of-california"],["Cali-Mex cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-california-latino-and-hispanic-influence"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Oyster","ingredient/oyster"]]},{"h":"More from Cuisine of California","links":[["Celery Victor","dish/celery-victor"],["Green goddess dressing","dish/green-goddess-dressing"],["Oysters Kirkpatrick","dish/oysters-kirkpatrick"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/bb/Hangtown_Burger.jpg/500px-Hangtown_Burger.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"John Herschell","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hangtown_Burger.jpg"}},"ingredient/coregonus-albula":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/coregonus-albula","n":"Coregonus albula","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Coregonus albula, known as the vendace or as the Least cisco, is a species of freshwater whitefish in the family Salmonidae. It is found in lakes in northern Europe, especially Finland, Latvia, Lithuania, Sweden, Russia and Estonia, and in some lakes of Norway, the United Kingdom, northern Germany, and Poland. It is also found in diluted brackish water in the Gulfs of Finland and Bothnia, both of which are in the Baltic Sea."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-coregonus-albula"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coregonus_albula"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Finnish cuisine","cuisine/finnish-cuisine",[["Kalakukko","dish/kalakukko"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["European perch","ingredient/european-perch"],["Fish fillet","ingredient/fish-fillet"],["Pork belly","ingredient/pork-belly"],["Rye flour","ingredient/rye-flour"],["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5b/Finnish_fried_vendace_%28Paistetut_muikut%29.jpg/500px-Finnish_fried_vendace_%28Paistetut_muikut%29.jpg","w":500,"h":332,"by":"V-7","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Finnish_fried_vendace_(Paistetut_muikut).jpg"}},"ingredient/agaricus-bisporus":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/agaricus-bisporus","n":"Agaricus bisporus","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 5 cuisines","intro":[["Agaricus bisporus, the cultivated mushroom, is a basidiomycete mushroom native to grasslands in Eurasia and North America. It is cultivated in more than 70 countries and is one of the most commonly and widely consumed mushrooms in the world. It has two color states while immature – white and brown – both of which have various names, with additional names for the mature state, such as chestnut, portobello, portabellini, button, cremini, and champignon de Paris (see Names below)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-agaricus-bisporus"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agaricus_bisporus"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":5,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Mie ayam","dish/mie-ayam"]]],["Korean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/korean-chinese-cuisine",[["Ulmyeon","dish/ulmyeon"]]],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine",[["Zapiekanka","dish/zapiekanka"]]],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Tom kha kai","dish/tom-kha-kai"]]],["Yoshoku","cuisine/yoshoku",[["Naporitan","dish/naporitan"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Noodle","ingredient/noodle"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Ketchup","ingredient/ketchup"],["Chinese cabbage","ingredient/chinese-cabbage"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Scallions","ingredient/scallions"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Sweet soy sauce","ingredient/sweet-soy-sauce"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/01/ChampignonMushroom.jpg/500px-ChampignonMushroom.jpg","w":500,"h":356,"by":"chris_73","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChampignonMushroom.jpg"}},"dish/nori":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/nori","n":"Nori","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Nori is a dried edible seaweed used in Japanese cuisine, usually made from species of the red algae genus Pyropia, including P. yezoensis and P. tenera. It has a strong and distinctive flavor, and is generally made into flat sheets and used to wrap rolls of sushi or onigiri (rice balls)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-nori"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nori"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Red alga","ingredient/red-alga"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","links":[["Bergh–Stoutenburgh House","dish/bergh-stoutenburgh-house"],["Chikuwa","dish/chikuwa"],["Curry bread","dish/curry-bread"],["Donburi","dish/donburi"],["Fukamushicha","dish/fukamushicha"],["Gyokuro","dish/gyokuro"],["Hitsumabushi","dish/hitsumabushi"],["Horumonyaki","dish/horumonyaki"],["Iekei ramen","dish/iekei-ramen"],["Inago no tsukudani","dish/inago-no-tsukudani"],["Itamae","dish/itamae"],["Izakaya","dish/izakaya"],["Japanese cheesecake","dish/japanese-cheesecake"],["Japanese noodles","dish/japanese-noodles"],["Japanese restaurant","dish/japanese-restaurant"],["Japanese tea","dish/japanese-tea"],["Jingisukan","dish/jingisukan"],["Kaki furai","dish/kaki-furai"],["Kamaboko","dish/kamaboko"],["Kōji (food)","dish/koji-food"],["Kusa mochi","dish/kusa-mochi"],["Kushikatsu","dish/kushikatsu"],["List of Japanese restaurants","dish/list-of-japanese-restaurants"],["Makizushi","dish/makizushi"],["Miso","dish/miso"],["Miso soup","dish/miso-soup"],["Monjayaki","dish/monjayaki"],["Nikujaga","dish/nikujaga"],["Nimono","dish/nimono"],["Oden","dish/oden"],["Onigiri","dish/onigiri"],["Osechi","dish/osechi"],["Oyakodon","dish/oyakodon"],["Rainbow roll","dish/rainbow-roll"],["Ramen","dish/ramen"],["Ryōtei","dish/ryotei"],["Sashimi","dish/sashimi"],["Sencha","dish/sencha"],["Shizuoka oden","dish/shizuoka-oden"],["Sichuan pepper","dish/sichuan-pepper"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/be/Nori.jpg/500px-Nori.jpg","w":500,"h":396,"by":"Alice Wiegand, (Lyzzy)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nori.jpg"}},"dish/paraguay-cheese":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/paraguay-cheese","n":"Paraguay cheese","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Paraguayan cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Paraguay cheese is a cows' milk cheese from Paraguay. It gives the Paraguayan cuisine a high value in calories and proteins, especially in the salted dishes recipes, very characteristic of the country and an important part of its culture."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-paraguay-cheese"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paraguay_cheese"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Paraguayan cuisine","cuisine/paraguayan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Milk","ingredient/milk"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Camila Made: Top 15 Paraguayan Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"camilamade.com","note":"Paraguayan recipe roundup on a Latin American home cooking blog.","url":"https://camilamade.com/top-15-paraguayan-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Paraguayan cuisine","links":[["Chipá","dish/chipa"],["Dulce de batata","dish/dulce-de-batata"],["Piracaldo","dish/piracaldo"],["Sopa paraguaya","dish/sopa-paraguaya"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/5/55/Queso_Paraguay_%28Kes%C3%BA_Paraguai%29.jpg","w":500,"h":468,"by":"Correadeb","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queso_Paraguay_(Kes%C3%BA_Paraguai).jpg"}},"dish/rui-be":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/rui-be","n":"Rui-be","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Ainu cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Rui-be or ruibe is a dish of the Ainu people of northern Japan, consisting of seafood that is frozen outdoors, sliced like sashimi, and served with soy sauce and water pepper."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-rui-be"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rui-be"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Ainu cuisine","cuisine/ainu-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Ainu cuisine","links":[["Munini-imo","dish/munini-imo"],["Ohaw","dish/ohaw"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4d/Salmon-Ruibe.jpg/500px-Salmon-Ruibe.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"MaedaAkihiko","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salmon-Ruibe.jpg"}},"ingredient/garnish-cooking":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/garnish-cooking","n":"Garnish (cooking)","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A garnish is an edible item or substance used as a decoration or embellishment for a prepared food dish or drink. Some garnishes are mainly for appearance, while others impart additional or contrasting flavors or textures, and may also serve cultural or symbolic functions. The notion of a garnish is distinct from that of a condiment, a prepared sauce added to another food item primarily for its flavor, but which does not serve a decorative function. A food item which is served with garnish may be described as being garni, French for \"garnished.\""]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-garnish-cooking"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garnish_(cooking)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Danish cuisine","cuisine/danish-cuisine",[["Smørrebrød","dish/sm-rrebr-d"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Cold cut","ingredient/cold-cut"],["Lunch meat","ingredient/lunch-meat"],["Rugbrød","ingredient/rugbr-d"],["Spread","ingredient/spread"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4b/Gyro_sandwich.jpg/500px-Gyro_sandwich.jpg","w":500,"h":368,"by":"jeffreyw","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gyro_sandwich.jpg"}}}